(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-5-cms-exam-5-master-exam-form-c"]={"title":"CMS I Exam 5 Master Exam — Form C","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 5","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Adverse ventricular remodeling in dilated cardiomyopathy gives the heart which shape?","choices":["Thick septal bulge","Narrow and tubular","Globular, or round","Normal-sized, big atria"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Adverse ventricular remodeling leads to a globular (globe or round) shaped heart, with an increase in total cardiac mass and dilated chambers. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"A 52-year-old man starts outpatient treatment for deep vein thrombosis with low molecular weight heparin injections overlapped with warfarin. The injections continue for at least five days and until his international normalized ratio is above which value?","choices":["Above 1","Above 2","Above 3","Above 4"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Parenteral therapy overlaps with warfarin for at least five days and until the international normalized ratio is above 2, when warfarin has reached its therapeutic effect and the heparin can stop. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"A 64-year-old woman with mitral valve endocarditis has 2 days of worsening breathlessness and orthopnea (breathlessness when lying flat). A new, loud regurgitant murmur is heard and crackles fill both lung bases. Which mechanism explains her heart failure?","choices":["Valvular dysfunction from infection","A toxic myocarditis from fever","Constriction from the pericardium","Fluid overload from antibiotics"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Congestive heart failure in infective endocarditis is due to valvular dysfunction, here from leaflet damage or chordal rupture, and it is the most common indication for surgery. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"A 62-year-old woman on chemotherapy for breast cancer comes to the emergency department with a calf 4 cm larger than the other, tenderness along the deep veins and no better explanation, so her Wells score is high. Her D-dimer, drawn at triage, is negative. Which action should be avoided?","choices":["Ordering a venous duplex ultrasound","Ending the work-up at the D-dimer","Measuring both calf circumferences","Asking about shortness of breath"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. With a high Wells score the D-dimer is skipped, or if it was drawn, a negative result does not end the evaluation: she still goes to venous duplex ultrasound. Stopping here risks missing a clot with high pretest probability. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"A 64-year-old man in atrial flutter has a heart rate of 150, a blood pressure of 70/40, and is confused. Which is the next step?","choices":["Oral rate control","Vagal maneuver","Anticoagulate for 21 days","Synchronized cardioversion"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Hemodynamically unstable atrial flutter (hypotension with confusion) is treated with synchronized cardioversion. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"A 62-year-old woman receiving chemotherapy through a central venous catheter has two days of right arm swelling and aching. The catheter injuring the vein wall is the endothelial trauma component of the Virchow triad. Which is the best diagnostic test?","choices":["D-dimer alone","Ankle-brachial index","Contrast ascending venography","Venous duplex ultrasound"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Venous duplex ultrasound of the involved extremity is the diagnostic test for deep vein thrombosis, including arm clots from central venous catheters. It has replaced venography, the old invasive gold standard. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"A 34-year-old man who injects heroin has 4 days of fever to 39.6 °C (103.3 °F), rigors and a cough. A new holosystolic murmur is heard at the left lower sternal border, and he looks toxic. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Community-acquired pneumonia","Acute viral pericarditis","Tricuspid valve endocarditis","Mitral valve endocarditis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. In people who inject drugs the tricuspid valve is the valve singled out (Staphylococcus aureus causes 80-90% of those infections); cough is linked to this group, and a holosystolic murmur at the left lower sternal border fits the tricuspid valve. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"A 27-year-old woman has had 20 minutes of sudden palpitations. Her heart rate is 188, blood pressure 122/76, and she is alert with no chest pain or breathlessness. The electrocardiogram shows a regular narrow complex tachycardia with no visible P waves. Which is the most appropriate first step?","choices":["Adenosine","Vagal maneuver","Synchronized cardioversion","Intravenous procainamide"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A stable, regular narrow complex tachycardia above 160 with hidden P waves is supraventricular tachycardia, and the first-line treatment when stable is a vagal maneuver, then adenosine. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"A 70-year-old woman being treated for an acute aortic dissection develops a new diastolic murmur and worsening breathlessness. Which complication explains these findings?","choices":["Aortic regurgitation","Pseudoaneurysm","Mitral stenosis","Carotid stenosis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Aortic dissection can cause aortic regurgitation through dilatation of the aortic root, producing a diastolic murmur and breathlessness. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 116"},{"q":"A 71-year-old woman with restrictive cardiomyopathy and marked biatrial enlargement has had palpitations for a week, and the electrocardiogram now shows atrial fibrillation. Which treatment reduces her risk of embolism?","choices":["Aggressive diuresis","Anticoagulation","A positive inotrope","Surgical myectomy"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Atrial fibrillation is common in restrictive cardiomyopathy because of atrial enlargement, it raises the risk of atrial thrombus, and anticoagulants may be needed to prevent embolism from atrial thrombi. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 99"},{"q":"A 45-year-old man has a heart rate of 122 with a normal P wave before every narrow QRS. His temperature is 39.2°C and he has a productive cough and crackles at the right base. He is alert with a blood pressure of 126/78. Which is the best treatment for his heart rate?","choices":["Treat the infection and fever","Vagal maneuver","Adenosine","Synchronized cardioversion"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A sinus tachycardia from fever and infection is an appropriate response to a pathologic process, and appropriate sinus tachycardia is managed by treating the underlying cause. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"A slow sinus rhythm that is causing hypotension and confusion is treated first with which drug?","choices":["Digoxin","Metoprolol","Adenosine","Atropine"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Acute, symptomatic, hemodynamically unstable bradycardia is treated first with atropine, then a temporary pacemaker if it fails; chronic symptomatic disease needs a permanent pacemaker. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"What is the common site of a pseudoaneurysm?","choices":["Popliteal artery after bypass","Infrarenal aorta in smokers","Carotid bulb after endarterectomy","Femoral artery after catheterization"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The common site is the femoral artery after cardiac catheterization, caused by laceration of the artery by the access needle. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 112"},{"q":"A 22-year-old man has recovered from mild viral myocarditis and feels well at discharge. He asks whether he needs any long-term follow-up with a cardiologist. Which later complication does follow-up watch for?","choices":["Restrictive cardiomyopathy","Dilated cardiomyopathy","Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy","Takotsubo cardiomyopathy"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Most patients with mild myocarditis recover completely, but one third subsequently develop dilated cardiomyopathy, and viral myocarditis accounts for about 20% of dilated cardiomyopathy cases. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"A 66-year-old woman with ovarian cancer has had three drainages for a recurrent pericardial effusion, and the latest echocardiogram shows a loculated collection behind the heart. Which procedure is most appropriate?","choices":["Diuretic therapy","Fourth needle tap","Thoracentesis","Pericardial window"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A pericardiectomy or pericardial window is performed for recurrent effusions and for loculated effusions, especially posterior ones that cannot be approached percutaneously. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"A 29-year-old woman has superficial thrombophlebitis of the forearm after an intravenous catheter and asks whether the clot can travel to her lungs. Which statement about her outlook is accurate?","choices":["Pulmonary embolism is the usual outcome","Serious complications are rare","Chronic venous insufficiency follows in most","Fatal embolism is likely without treatment"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Superficial phlebitis and thrombophlebitis rarely cause serious complications and rarely embolize, which distinguishes them from deep vein thrombosis, where pulmonary embolism is the major complication. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 78"},{"q":"A 68-year-old woman has atrial fibrillation found at a routine visit. Her heart rate is 84, she has no symptoms of instability, and her blood pressure is 128/76. Which is the most appropriate rate management?","choices":["Oral rate control","Intravenous rate control","Atropine","Synchronized cardioversion"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A hemodynamically stable patient in atrial fibrillation without rapid ventricular response is managed with rate control by mouth, with anticoagulation guided by stroke risk. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"A 30-year-old cyclist with no symptoms has a rhythm in which the PR interval lengthens beat by beat until a QRS complex is dropped, after which the cycle starts again. Echocardiogram is normal. Which is the most appropriate management?","choices":["Transcutaneous pacing","Catheter ablation","Permanent pacemaker","No treatment needed"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Asymptomatic Mobitz type I block needs no treatment; a permanent pacemaker is considered only for debilitating symptoms or heart failure symptoms. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"A 78-year-old man with recurrent urinary tract infections has aortic valve endocarditis, and his blood cultures grow Enterococcus faecalis. Which regimen is most appropriate?","choices":["Nafcillin plus rifampin","Cefazolin alone","Oral amoxicillin once","Ampicillin plus gentamicin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Enterococcal endocarditis is treated with ampicillin (or penicillin G) plus gentamicin, ampicillin plus ceftriaxone, or vancomycin plus gentamicin. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"An 82-year-old woman taking digoxin for heart failure has nausea and fatigue. Her heart rate is 48 and regular, with narrow QRS complexes and retrograde P waves (inverted) just after each QRS in II, III and aVF. Which is the most likely cause of this rhythm?","choices":["Hyperthyroidism","Missed digoxin doses","An accessory pathway","Digoxin toxicity"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A slow regular narrow rhythm with inverted P waves after the QRS is a junctional rhythm, and digoxin toxicity is a listed cause. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"A 35-year-old woman has 3 days of sharp substernal chest pain that improves when she sits forward. Her vital signs are normal, and no rub is heard on careful auscultation. Which additional finding would complete the clinical diagnosis of acute pericarditis?","choices":["Widespread ST elevation on electrocardiogram","An elevated B-type natriuretic peptide","A troponin above the reference range","Pericardial calcification on radiograph"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The diagnosis needs two of four criteria: sharp substernal chest pain, a friction rub, suggestive electrocardiogram changes (widespread ST elevation) and a new or worsening effusion. Her pain plus the electrocardiogram would make two. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"Which Virchow triad component can be inherited or acquired?","choices":["Endothelial trauma","Hypercoagulable state","All three equally","Venous stasis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A hypercoagulable state can be inherited (factor V Leiden, prothrombin gene mutation, protein C or S or antithrombin deficiency) or acquired (cancer, estrogen, pregnancy). — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"A 45-year-old man who grew up in rural Bolivia has months of fatigue and exertional breathlessness. His heart rate is 38 and the electrocardiogram shows P waves completely dissociated from slow, wide QRS complexes. Which infection is a named cause of his conduction disease?","choices":["Tuberculosis","Lyme disease","Chagas disease","Malaria"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Chagas disease is a named cause of complete heart block; it is a parasitic infection endemic in parts of Latin America such as rural Bolivia. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"A 64-year-old man with claudication, diabetes and continued smoking is taking a statin. Which low-density lipoprotein target applies to him?","choices":["Below 70 mg/dL","Below 100 mg/dL","Below 130 mg/dL","Below 160 mg/dL"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Lipid-lowering therapy in peripheral artery disease targets a low-density lipoprotein below 100 mg/dL, and below 70 mg/dL for high-risk patients such as those with diabetes or continued smoking. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"A 42-year-old woman has symptomatic varicose veins that did not improve with stockings. She prefers a nonsurgical procedure in which a solution is injected to scar and close the veins. Which procedure fits?","choices":["Sclerotherapy","Venous bypass","Radiofrequency ablation","Vein stripping"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Sclerotherapy is the injection of a sclerosing agent to fibrose and obliterate the veins, one of the interventions for varicose veins along with stripping and catheter-based ablation. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"A 69-year-old man has an asymptomatic abdominal aortic aneurysm measuring 4.7 cm on his latest ultrasound. Which referral is appropriate?","choices":["Emergency surgery this evening","None until it reaches 5.5 cm","Vascular referral, for observation","Cardiology, for echocardiography"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Aneurysms of 4.5 cm or larger warrant referral to a vascular team for observation and assessment, with urgent referral whenever the patient has aneurysmal pain, whatever the size. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 100"},{"q":"A 62-year-old man with fever, a new murmur and positive blood cultures is stable. A transthoracic study is limited by his body habitus. Which imaging test best detects vegetations and defines the extent of disease?","choices":["Repeat transthoracic study","Chest radiograph","Transesophageal echocardiogram","Coronary angiography"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Transesophageal echocardiography is highly sensitive and specific, detects vegetations on the valve leaflets and defines the extent of disease. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"A 16-year-old boy collapses briefly during a basketball game and recovers within a minute. Blood pressure and heart rate are normal. There is a crescendo-decrescendo systolic murmur at the left sternal border in the fourth intercostal space that does not radiate to the carotids; it grows louder when he stands and softer when he squats. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Restrictive cardiomyopathy","Acute myocarditis","Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy","Dilated cardiomyopathy"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Exertional syncope in an adolescent with an outflow murmur at the left sternal border that does not reach the carotids, louder with standing and softer with squatting, is obstructive hypertrophic cardiomyopathy. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"Which patient with recurrent pericarditis is described as a candidate for anti-interleukin-1 therapy?","choices":["One in tamponade right now","One who is steroid-dependent","One with a first, mild episode","One with a normal C-reactive protein"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Recurrent or colchicine-resistant disease, or steroid-dependent disease, with an inflammatory phenotype makes anakinra or rilonacept the preferred option. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"Which oral prophylaxis fits a patient with a penicillin allergy?","choices":["Azithromycin","Amoxicillin","Ampicillin","Clindamycin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Penicillin-allergic patients who can take oral medication receive azithromycin or clarithromycin, cephalexin (if no anaphylaxis, angioedema or urticaria) or doxycycline. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"How is polymorphic ventricular tachycardia treated, even when the patient has a pulse?","choices":["Intravenous lidocaine alone","Observation on a monitor","Defibrillation","Synchronized cardioversion"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Polymorphic ventricular tachycardia is never truly stable, only a short-lived transitional state, so it is defibrillated even if the patient looks well. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"Adenosine slows a tachycardia and reveals atrial fibrillation with Wolff-Parkinson-White. Which drug is used?","choices":["Intravenous magnesium","Intravenous atropine","A second dose of adenosine","Intravenous procainamide"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. When slowing from adenosine reveals atrial fibrillation with Wolff-Parkinson-White, intravenous procainamide is used and adenosine is not repeated; adenosine and the other nodal blockers leave the accessory pathway unopposed. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"A 66-year-old woman with aortic valve endocarditis has a transesophageal echocardiogram that shows an abscess around the valve ring. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Surgical evaluation","Repeat echo in 6 months","Oral step-down now","Shorten antibiotics"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Abscess is an indication for surgery, alongside heart failure from valve dysfunction, infection not resolving on appropriate antibiotics and fungal infection, so cardiothoracic surgery evaluates her. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"A 64-year-old woman with hypertension has a carotid bruit found on routine examination. She has never had neurologic symptoms. Duplex shows 35% stenosis. Which is the most appropriate management?","choices":["Medical management","Carotid endarterectomy","Carotid stenting","Digital subtraction angiography"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Medical management is advised for symptomatic and asymptomatic patients with low-grade stenosis under 50%: antiplatelet therapy, control of hypertension, diabetes and lipids, smoking cessation, diet, exercise and weight reduction. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 75"},{"q":"Which aortic condition can also shed emboli that cause blue toe syndrome?","choices":["Pseudoaneurysm of the femoral","Abdominal aortic aneurysm","Carotid stenosis","Aortic regurgitation"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A symptomatic, unruptured abdominal aortic aneurysm can produce embolic phenomena leading to blue toe syndrome, besides pain, early satiety and groin pain. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 89"},{"q":"A 38-year-old woman with obstructive hypertrophic cardiomyopathy still has disabling breathlessness despite a beta blocker and later verapamil. Echocardiogram shows systolic anterior motion (the anterior mitral leaflet pulled into the outflow tract) with mitral regurgitation. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Escalating loop diuretics","Surgical septal myectomy","Permanent pacemaker alone","Long-term nitrate therapy"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Surgical myectomy excises part of the septal myocardium causing outflow obstruction, and the mitral regurgitation from systolic anterior motion may resolve with myectomy alone, without valve replacement. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 79"},{"q":"A 66-year-old woman with chronic obstructive lung disease is admitted with an exacerbation. She is confused, and the monitor shows runs of skipped beats from frequent early, wide QRS complexes without P waves. Her arterial oxygen tension is low. Which underlying problem should be corrected first?","choices":["Her hypoxemia","Her blood pressure","Her body temperature","Her blood glucose"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Hypoxemia is a main cause of premature ventricular contractions, along with electrolyte imbalance, so her low oxygen should be corrected first. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"A 44-year-old man began having pleuritic (worse with a deep breath) pericardial pain 8 weeks ago. Despite treatment the symptoms have never fully gone away, with no symptom-free interval. Which term best describes his course?","choices":["Recurrent pericarditis","Incessant pericarditis","Chronic pericarditis","Effusive-constrictive disease"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Symptoms persisting beyond 4 to 6 weeks and up to 3 months without a sustained remission define incessant pericarditis; past 3 months the label becomes chronic. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"A 45-year-old woman taking warfarin for a mechanical valve presents with 2 days of sharp chest pain eased by leaning forward, widespread ST elevation and a small effusion. She is afebrile and her blood pressure is 128/78. Which is the most appropriate disposition?","choices":["Discharge with clinic follow-up","Outpatient cardiac imaging next week","Admit her to the hospital","Discharge after one dose of ibuprofen"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Patients on oral anticoagulants are among the higher-risk groups for acute pericarditis (with fever, subacute course, tamponade signs, large effusion, immunosuppression, trauma, raised troponin) and potentially need hospitalization. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"A 71-year-old man with severe chronic obstructive pulmonary disease is admitted with an exacerbation and respiratory failure. His pulse is 128 and irregular. The electrocardiogram shows a P wave before every QRS, with at least three different P-wave shapes in lead II. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Atrial fibrillation","Atrial flutter, variable block","Focal atrial tachycardia","Multifocal atrial tachycardia"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. An irregular tachycardia with a P wave for every QRS and at least three distinct P-wave shapes in one lead is multifocal atrial tachycardia, usually from severe lung disease or heart failure in an acutely ill patient. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"A 75-year-old man with peripheral artery disease has a hard, dry, black distal toe with a clear line between healthy and dead tissue. There is no swelling, moisture or fever. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Wet gangrene","Gas gangrene","Dry gangrene","Atheroembolism"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Dry gangrene has a hard, dry texture, usually involves the distal toes, shows clear demarcation between healthy and necrotic tissue, and is common in patients with peripheral artery disease. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"In unstable Mobitz type II block, when medications are not used or do not work, which is the emergent measure?","choices":["Synchronized cardioversion","Defibrillation","Vagal maneuvers","Transcutaneous pacing"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Transcutaneous pacing, with pads on the chest, is the emergent measure for unstable Mobitz type II block; it bridges the patient to a permanent pacemaker. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"A 38-year-old woman has three months of worsening breathlessness on exertion and ankle swelling. Dilated cardiomyopathy is suspected, but her 12-lead electrocardiogram is completely normal. Which interpretation of the normal tracing is most accurate?","choices":["It is what most dilated cardiomyopathy shows","It excludes every cause of heart failure","It makes dilated cardiomyopathy very unlikely","It points to restrictive disease instead"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The electrocardiogram is usually abnormal in dilated cardiomyopathy, showing atrial fibrillation, premature ventricular contractions or left bundle branch block, so a normal tracing almost rules the diagnosis out. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"An 86-year-old woman with end-stage cardiomyopathy has breathlessness at rest, edema and fatigue despite maximal tolerated therapy. She is not a transplant or device candidate and says she wants comfort at home. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Add an assist device urgently","Refer for heart transplant listing","Discuss goals of care and hospice","Stop all of her symptom medicines"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Palliative care in cardiomyopathy covers symptom management of dyspnea, fatigue and edema, advance care planning and goals-of-care discussions, support for caregivers, and hospice referral in end-stage disease. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 107"},{"q":"An 81-year-old man has known tachy-brady syndrome (a fast rhythm alternating with slow rates and pauses) and asks which fast rhythm is behind his palpitations. An ambulatory monitor is arranged. Which rhythm is it most likely to record during the fast episodes?","choices":["Ventricular tachycardia","Junctional rhythm","Atrial flutter","Atrial fibrillation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. In tachy-brady syndrome the tachyarrhythmia that alternates with bradycardia or pauses is most commonly atrial fibrillation. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"A 53-year-old man on the cardiology ward develops jugular venous distension and a blood pressure of 88/60 a day after a catheter procedure. Before drainage, which bedside triad should be specifically checked for?","choices":["Hypotension, neck veins, muffled sounds","Alternans, Kussmaul sign, a knock","Hypertension, bradycardia, apnea","Fever, a new murmur and splinters"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The Beck triad is decreased arterial pressure, distended jugular veins and distant (muffled) heart sounds, the three bedside signs of cardiac tamponade to check before drainage. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"A 26-year-old man with suspected viral myocarditis has an elevated troponin and normal coronary arteries. His cardiologist wants to see how much of the heart muscle is inflamed without an invasive procedure. Which test shows the extent of inflammation?","choices":["Right heart catheterization with pressure tracings","Gadolinium-enhanced cardiac magnetic resonance imaging","Exercise echocardiography with treadmill testing","Ambulatory rhythm monitoring for forty-eight hours"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Gadolinium-enhanced magnetic resonance imaging is part of the myocarditis work-up because the gadolinium improves visibility of the inflammation and shows its extent. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"A 34-year-old woman without diabetes has ulcers on the posterior aspect of both lower legs. Her pedal pulses are strong, and there are no varicose veins, brown pigmentation or leg swelling. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Arterial ulcer","Venous ulcer","Erythema induratum","Diabetic neuropathic ulcer"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Bilateral ulcers on the posterior aspect of the lower leg, with intact pulses and no venous skin changes, is the pattern listed for erythema induratum in the differential of leg ulcers. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"A 61-year-old woman with long-standing pulmonary hypertension from scleroderma has a right bundle branch block on her electrocardiogram. Which of her conditions is a named cause of this block?","choices":["Acid reflux","Osteoporosis","Pulmonary hypertension","Raynaud phenomenon"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Pulmonary hypertension is a named cause of right bundle branch block, by chronically straining the right ventricle and its conduction system. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"A 28-year-old woman with a stab wound to the abdomen arrives pulseless after losing a large amount of blood. The monitor shows an organized narrow rhythm. Compressions are underway. Besides compressions and epinephrine, which problem must be addressed urgently?","choices":["Hypothermia","Hypovolemia","Toxin ingestion","Hyperkalemia"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The two leading causes of pulseless electrical activity in adults are hypovolemia and hypoxia; her massive blood loss is hypovolemia that must be corrected. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"A 67-year-old man with atrial fibrillation is being discharged after treatment of an embolic leg occlusion. Which warning symptoms should prompt him to return immediately?","choices":["Dry, shiny skin on the shins","Mild ankle swelling in the evening","Sudden pain, pallor, coldness, numbness","Calf cramps after long walks"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The six P's of arterial assessment (pain, pallor, pulselessness, paresthesia, paralysis and poikilothermia) mark acute limb ischemia, a vascular emergency that needs immediate care. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man has a continuous bruit and a palpable thrill (a vibration felt over the skin) over the left femoral region, with mild leg swelling. He has not had dialysis and has no trauma history. His only recent procedure was an endovascular repair of an iliac artery performed through the left groin. Which is the most likely cause?","choices":["Groin access for the procedure","Congenital vascular malformation","Progressive superficial venous reflux","Surgical creation for dialysis access"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Lower extremity arteriovenous fistulas are most commonly iatrogenic, caused by use of the groin femoral vessels for percutaneous arterial and venous access during endovascular procedures. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 82"},{"q":"Which electrocardiogram changes occur in Takotsubo (stress) cardiomyopathy?","choices":["Left bundle branch block only","Low QRS voltage in all leads","Inferior and lateral Q waves","ST elevation and T wave inversion"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The electrocardiogram shows ST segment elevation and T wave inversion, which is why the syndrome is managed like acute coronary syndrome until catheterization. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 103"},{"q":"A 63-year-old man has an incidental 4.0 cm ascending thoracic aortic aneurysm on a chest radiograph and follow-up imaging. He has no symptoms. Which surveillance plan is appropriate?","choices":["Abdominal ultrasound every three years with no other imaging","Computed tomography or magnetic resonance every 3 to 5 years","Computed tomography or magnetic resonance every 6 to 12 months","Open repair with aortic root replacement within the month"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Thoracic aneurysms under 4.5 cm are followed with computed tomography or magnetic resonance imaging every three to five years; 4.5 to 5.4 cm moves to every six to 12 months. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 109"},{"q":"A 57-year-old man survives an out-of-hospital cardiac arrest from pulseless ventricular tachycardia. After stabilization no reversible cause is found. Which device is most appropriate before discharge?","choices":["Permanent pacemaker","Biventricular pacemaker","Implantable defibrillator","Implantable loop recorder"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Once stabilized after life-threatening ventricular tachycardia or fibrillation, most patients need an implantable cardioverter defibrillator for secondary prevention of sudden cardiac death. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"Why is a nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drug approached with caution in older adults with pericarditis?","choices":["It appears on the Beers list","It causes drug-induced lupus","It raises the risk of tamponade","It prolongs the QT interval"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. In older adults, nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs appear on the Beers list of potentially inappropriate medications and call for caution; colchicine may need a dose reduction. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"A 69-year-old woman being evaluated for endocarditis had a soft systolic murmur documented for 10 years. Today the murmur sounds a little louder. How does this change count under the modified Duke criteria?","choices":["It meets the major criterion","It confirms definite endocarditis","It is an immunologic phenomenon","It does not meet the major criterion"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. New valvular regurgitation counts as endocardial involvement, but an increase or change in a preexisting murmur is not sufficient. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"A 63-year-old woman is diagnosed with constrictive pericarditis. She had mantle-field radiotherapy for Hodgkin lymphoma 25 years ago and is otherwise well. Which feature of her history most worsens her long-term prognosis?","choices":["Her female sex","Her lack of other illness","Her cured lymphoma","Her prior chest radiation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Etiology is an important determinant of survival in constrictive pericarditis, and prior ionizing radiation is associated with the worst survival rates. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"A 54-year-old woman who started a new antibiotic three days ago faints briefly. Her electrocardiogram before the event showed a markedly prolonged QT. The monitor now shows a wide QRS tachycardia whose peaks rise and fall so that the complexes seem to twist around the baseline. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Torsades de pointes","Atrial flutter","Monomorphic ventricular tachycardia","Coarse ventricular fibrillation"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Polymorphic ventricular tachycardia whose QRS complexes appear to twist around the baseline, on a background of a long QT, is torsades de pointes; most cases come from acquired long QT, often drug-induced. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"A 6-year-old with repaired congenital heart disease has 3 weeks of low-grade fever, fatigue, joint aches and weight loss. A new murmur is heard. Which organism most commonly causes endocarditis in this age group?","choices":["Enterococci","Viridans group streptococci","Group B streptococci","Candida species"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Children over 1 year with congenital heart disease develop indolent viridans group streptococcal disease; Staphylococcus aureus comes next. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 40"}]};
